      "Language": "eng",
      "Abstract": {"AbstractText": [
        {
          "NlmCategory": "OBJECTIVE",
          "Label": "OBJECTIVE",
          "content": "We investigated whether levels of intercellular adhesion molecule 1 (ICAM-1) antigen shed into the circulation increase during acute Kawasaki disease (KD). We also compared ICAM-1 levels in acute KD with those in anaphylactoid purpura (AP) and in measles."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "METHODS",
          "Label": "METHODS",
          "content": "Serum ICAM-1 levels were measured by a double-determinant immunoassay using 2 monoclonal antibodies in the FAST (Falcon assay screening test) system. Serum levels of tumor necrosis factor alpha (TNF alpha) were measured by a specific and sensitive sandwich enzyme immunoassay."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "RESULTS",
          "Label": "RESULTS",
          "content": "Patients with KD, but not those with AP or measles, had increased levels of shed ICAM-1 antigen in serum samples obtained during acute stages. Moreover, during the acute stage, KD patients with coronary artery lesions (CAL) had still higher levels of shed ICAM-1 than did those without CAL. We found a positive correlation between serum levels of shed ICAM-1 and levels of TNF alpha during acute KD."
        },
        {
          "NlmCategory": "CONCLUSIONS",
          "Label": "CONCLUSION",
          "content": "Our findings suggest that the serum ICAM-1 level is an important immunologic parameter for determining the severity of vascular damage during acute KD."
        }
      ]},
